{#- THE ACTIVATION MATRIX — replaces the two fixed activation tools that
    lived at this URL and at /members/activacion-ciclismo/ (both retired
    Aug 13, 2026 on Iván's call). Home doc: `activation-matrix.md`.

    Three questions → one of 14 routines (sport × moment × equipment). Not 12:
    swimming has three equipment options where run and bike have two, so the
    grid is 4 + 4 + 6. The tightness axis is v2 and deliberately absent; see
    the home doc.

    Structure: one exercise LIBRARY, and 14 routines that are just lists of ids.
    That is the whole point of the design — lists and a lookup, never hand-
    written routines, because the exercises they share must not drift into
    slightly different cues.

    *** v1.1, Sept 3, 2026 — SWIMMING added as a third sport, Iván's call, and
    it forced two structural changes rather than one extra button:

    1. The equipment question is now SPORT-AWARE. A mini-band is the right
       question for run and bike and the wrong object for swimming, where the
       useful kit is a long band and a stick. `EQUIP` holds the options per
       sport and the third question re-renders when the sport changes.
    2. Phase names moved OUT of build() and INTO each routine. Run and bike go
       floor → knees → standing and never back down; swimming is done on a wet
       pool deck in the few minutes before the session and does not follow that
       progression. The engine already takes phase names from the data, so this
       cost nothing — but it means `R` entries now carry `phases`, not
       floor/knees/stand.

    Honest note on why swimming lives here rather than on its own page: the
    exercise overlap with run and bike is almost nil (cat-cow, child's pose).
    The merge is justified by product coherence — the library had two
    activation entries once and consolidating them was deliberate — NOT by code
    reuse. Do not let a future pass "simplify" this by splitting it back out.

    *** CLINICAL: stick DISLOCATES are deliberately not here. The source doc
    called them indispensable for opening the anterior capsule; competitive
    swimmers are typically lax there, not stiff, and short of internal rotation
    — which is what /members/hombro/ is built around. What ships instead is a
    capped pass-through that stops at the first compensation. Same class of
    finding as the sleeper stretch on the shoulder tool. Do not restore
    dislocates. ***

    *** GUARDRAIL: methodology.md says Iván does not teach swimming. The catch
    simulation primes the feel of pressure; it must never become stroke
    instruction. Read the cue before editing it. ***

    The engine (activation-tool.js) is unchanged. It boots off
    window.ACTIVATION_DATA, so this page builds that object after the athlete
    answers and only then injects the engine script — which is why the partial
    is included with `matrixMode`.

    *** EVERY NOTE ABOUT THIS PAGE BELONGS IN THIS BLOCK. Almost the entire page
    is inside {% raw %}, and Nunjucks comments are NOT comments in there — they
    are literal text, and they render to the reader. Two did exactly that on
    Sept 3, 2026 and Iván saw them on the live page. There is now a build check
    asserting no `{#` survives into any built HTML, but the simpler rule is:
    put the note here, or use an HTML comment. ***

    Two notes that were inline and had to move here:

    - The setup-head paragraph is SPORT-NEUTRAL on purpose, because it is read
      before the sport is chosen. It used to promise "del suelo a estar de pie,
      sin volver a bajar", which stopped being true of every routine the moment
      swimming arrived. The per-sport version of that sentence is each
      routine's own `subtitle`.
    - #equipOpts is left empty in the markup: its options AND its label are
      written by the setup script from EQUIP[sport], so that switching sport
      changes the question and not just the answer. -#}
